<comment datatype='string'>Corrinoid iron-sulfur proteins (CoFeSP) are functional in the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=CODH-PWY&quot;&gt;reductive acetyl coenzyme A pathway I (homoacetogenic bacteria)&lt;/a&gt; of autotrophic carbon fixation in various bacteria and archaea, where they are essential for the biosynthesis of acetyl-CoA.
&lt;p&gt;
The protein from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-129958&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Carboxydothermus hydrogenoformans&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; contains two cofactors - &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=Co-a-5-methoxy-benzimidazole-cobamides&quot; title=&quot;&lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=Cobamides''&gt;A cobamide&lt;/a&gt; is a corrinoid molecule based on the structure of &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-20843''&gt;cobamide&lt;/a&gt;, a cobalt-corrinoid hexaamide that is formed from &lt;a href=''/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=COBINAMIDE''&gt;cob(I)inamide&lt;/a&gt; by the attachment of an &amp;alpha;-D-ribofuranose 3-phosphate residue at the 2 position of the aminopropanol chain.&amp;#10;&amp;#10;Biologically active forms of cobamides contain an upper (&amp;beta;) and a lower (&amp;alpha;) axial ligands. The most common upper axial ligand are either a methyl group or a 5'-deoxyadenosyl group, although some other forms exist. More variability is found with the lower axial ligand, where more that 16 different forms have been described. This class includes all cobamides that have a 5-methoxy-6-methylbenzimidazole group as their lower axial ligand.&quot;&gt;5-methoxybenzimidazolylcobamide&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&amp;object=CPD-7&quot; title=&quot;Iron-sulfur centers consist of nonheme iron complexed to sulfur in several ways.&amp;#10;Iron-sulfur clusters are prosthetic groups of many proteins, which function as intracellular electron carriers&amp;#10;with a low reduction potential.&amp;#10;In addition to their roles in electron transfer reactions, iron-sulfur clusters are also known to participate in&amp;#10;the activation of substrates, the stabilization of radicals and structures, the protection of proteins from enzymes&amp;#10;and the storage of iron and sulfur.&amp;#10;In addition, they act as sensors of iron, dioxygen, the superoxide ion, and possibly nitric oxide, and participate&amp;#10;in gene expression [&lt;a href=''http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16466637''&gt;Park06&lt;/a&gt;].&amp;#10;&amp;#10;[4Fe-4S] iron-sulfur clusters feature four iron ions and four sulfide ions placed at the vertices of a cubane-type structure.  The Fe centers are typically further coordinated by cysteinyl ligands. &amp;#10;&amp;#10;Two main types of [4Fe-4S] iron-sulfur clusters are low-potential (bacterial-type) clusters and high-potential (HiPIP) clusters. The bacterial clusters shift between the oxidation states +1 and +2, while the HiPIP clusters shift between the oxidation states +2 and +3.&amp;#10;&amp;#10;In some enzymes the [4Fe-4S] iron-sulfur clusters bind substrates and are thus classified as enzyme cofactors. For example, in radical SAM enzymes the cluster binds and reduces S-adenosylmethionine, generating a radical that interacts with the enzyme's substrate. In a few cases, as in &lt;a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-2.8.1.8''&gt;EC 2.8.1.8, lipoyl synthase&lt;/a&gt;, the cluster serves as the source of sulfur atoms for a sulfuration reaction [&lt;a href=''http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/29051382''&gt;McCarthy17&lt;/a&gt;].&quot;&gt;a [4Fe-4S] iron-sulfur cluster&lt;/a&gt;. The cobamide is bound in a base-off state, with neither the 5-methoxybenzimidazolyl group nor a nitrogen atom from a protein side chain coordinating the cobalt [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16983091&quot;&gt;Svetlitchnaia06&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
CoFeSP acts in two methylation reactions: the transfer of a methyl group from methyltransferase-bound methyltetrahydrofolate to the cob(I)amide of CoFeSP, during which the cobalt is oxidized to the (III) state (&lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-2.1.1.258&quot;&gt;EC 2.1.1.258, 5-methyltetrahydrofolate&amp;mdash;corrinoid/iron-sulfur protein &lt;em&gt;Co&lt;/em&gt;-methyltransferase&lt;/a&gt;), and the transfer of the methyl group of methyl-cob(III)amide to the reduced Ni-Ni-[4Fe-4S] active site cluster of &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-2.3.1.169&quot;&gt;EC 2.3.1.169, CO-methylating acetyl-CoA synthase&lt;/a&gt;, during which the cobalt is restored to its active (I) state [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16983091&quot;&gt;Svetlitchnaia06&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
More recent findings suggest that the protein may also be involved as a methyl donor in methionine biosynthesis by MesB-type core methionine synthases [&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2020.04.21.054031v2&quot;&gt;Price20&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The protein is heterodimeric, consisting of two tightly interacting subunits. The large CfsA subunit comprises three domains, of which the N-terminal domain binds the [4Fe-4S] cluster, the middle domain is a (&amp;beta;&amp;alpha;)&lt;sub&gt;8&lt;/sub&gt;-barrel, and the C-terminal domain shows an open fold and binds Cobeta-aqua-(5,6-dimethylbenzimidazolylcobamide) in a &quot;base-off&quot; state without a protein ligand at the cobalt ion. The small CfsB subunit also displays a (&amp;beta;&amp;alpha;)&lt;sub&gt;8&lt;/sub&gt;-barrel fold and interacts with the upper side of the corrin macrocycle [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16983091&quot;&gt;Svetlitchnaia06&lt;/a&gt;].
</comment>
